<hr class="rule">

<!-- ══════════════ ARGUMENT 3 ══════════════ -->
<div class="arg-banner green reveal" id="arg3" data-num="03">
  <span class="arg-label">Argument Three</span>
  <h2>AI Empowers the People Who Learn to Use It Well</h2>
</div>

<!-- ══ IMAGE BREAK 4 ══ -->
<div class="img-break reveal">
  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1485827404703-89b55fcc595e?w=1600&auto=format&fit=crop&q=75" alt="Robot and human collaboration technology future">
  <span class="img-break-caption">Human and machine · working together</span>
</div>

<section id="arg3-body">
<div class="prose">
  <p class="reveal">The most important thing I have learned about AI this semester is not a statistic. It is a principle: AI is an amplifier. Whatever you bring to it, it makes more of. If you bring laziness, you get laziness at scale, polished-sounding output with no real thinking behind it. If you bring genuine effort and genuine questions, you get something rare: a tool that helps you think further, faster, and more clearly than you could alone.</p>

  <p class="reveal">I came into this semester with a relatively simple view of AI. It felt like a novelty, useful for studying, maybe a little risky. Over the course of the semester, working through research on AI and the brain, AI in education, AI in business, and AI in global politics, my view has sharpened considerably. AI is not just automating tasks. It is automating parts of reasoning, which fundamentally changes what human beings are responsible for. The highest-value human skills are no longer information recall or routine execution. They are judgment, creativity, leadership, and the ability to ask the right questions. AI handles everything else.</p>

  <div class="slide-card reveal">
    <span class="sc-label">From the Presentations</span>
    <div class="sc-title">From Tool to Agent: AI in Everyday Hands</div>
    <p class="sc-body">AI has shifted from a specialized corporate resource to something ordinary people can build for themselves: homework assistance, LinkedIn outreach agents, email writers, scheduling tools. These uses expand human speeds and capabilities. The focus must be on workflow integration and on training AI agents properly in order to sustain productivity and genuine skill, rather than undermine them.</p>
    <span class="sc-attr">(Erin and Tess, "AI in Business," WRT 205 Presentation)</span>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">As a student-athlete, the way I use AI is a direct reflection of this principle. I do not use it to avoid the hard parts of learning. I use it to make the hard parts possible given real constraints. When I have ninety minutes after an evening practice and a chapter of business analytics to absorb, I cannot afford to spend forty-five minutes re-reading a paragraph that isn't clicking. I ask ChatGPT to explain it differently. When I need to write a response paper the next morning, I do not ask it to write the paper. I use it to stress-test my argument before I sit down to write, to find the holes in my logic before my professor does. That is not offloading cognition. That is using a tool to think more rigorously.</p>

  <div class="pullquote reveal">
    <p>How do we use AI responsibly without harming learning, thinking, or human connection?</p>
    <cite>Janice and Katelyn, "Artificial Intelligence in Education," WRT 205 Presentation</cite>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">A 2025 study from Microsoft Research and Carnegie Mellon University backs this up directly. Researchers surveyed 319 knowledge workers and found that the more confident someone was in AI's ability to handle a task, the less critical thinking they actually did. The study called this a "perceived enaction of critical thinking," meaning people felt like they were thinking carefully while actually disengaging. That pattern was strongest on routine, lower-stakes tasks, which is exactly the kind of work most people use AI for daily (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-critical-thinking-self-reported-reductions-in-cognitive-effort-and-confidence-effects-from-a-survey-of-knowledge-workers/" target="_blank" class="cite-link">Tankelevitch et al.</a>). That distinction matters. When we trust AI too completely, we stop engaging. Over time, the habits of rigorous thought weaken. But the solution is not to abandon the tool. It is to use it with enough intentionality that you remain the one doing the thinking, with AI as the collaborator rather than the replacement.</p>

  <p class="reveal">At the largest scale, this principle holds across nations. The Atlantic Council published a piece in January 2026 outlining eight ways AI will shape geopolitics in the year ahead. The central argument is that the competition between the US and China over AI dominance is intensifying, with both nations racing to control chips, computing power, and data infrastructure. The piece notes that by end of 2026, global AI governance is likely to be "global in form but geopolitical in substance," meaning the rules will exist on paper while the real power remains with whoever controls the technology. The concept of Sovereign AI frames a nation's ability to build AI using its own infrastructure as a direct measure of independence and power. Nations and individuals who treat AI as something to master rather than something to fear will be the ones positioned to shape what comes next (<a href="https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/dispatches/eight-ways-ai-will-shape-geopolitics-in-2026/" target="_blank" class="cite-link">Atlantic Council Technology Programs</a>).</p>

  <p class="reveal">The students who graduate from college in 2026 knowing how to use AI thoughtfully, who can direct it, evaluate its outputs critically, and integrate it into complex workflows, will not be replaced by AI. They will be the ones deciding what AI does. That is a profoundly valuable place to be. Getting there requires doing exactly what too many people are afraid to do: engaging seriously with the technology, learning its limits, and developing the judgment to know when to trust it and when to push past it.</p>
</div>
</section>

