<!-- ══════════════ CONCLUSION ══════════════ -->
<section id="conclusion">
<div class="prose">
  <span class="eyebrow reveal">Conclusion</span>
  <div class="accent-bar reveal reveal-delay-1"></div>
  <h2 class="reveal reveal-delay-1">The Tool Is in Our Hands</h2>

  <p class="reveal reveal-delay-2">I want to return to where I started: 10:30 at night, a student-athlete with ninety minutes, a chapter to master, and a practice session's worth of exhaustion in my legs. That is not a glamorous picture. But it is a true one. And it is the picture that convinced me, more than any research paper or data point, that AI matters. Used well, it has the power to make people more capable than the circumstances they are handed.</p>

  <p class="reveal">This essay has made three interconnected arguments. AI levels the playing field in education, making quality learning support available to students who never had access to it before. AI makes workers and businesses more capable by handling what is routine so that humans can focus on what is genuinely human. And AI empowers the individuals who take the time to learn how to use it with intention and rigor. All three arguments are grounded in my own experience, in the research my class explored this semester, and in real data about how AI is actually being used across industries and institutions right now.</p>

  <p class="reveal">The concerns about AI are real and worth taking seriously. Cognitive dependency, algorithmic bias, data privacy risks, the pace of job transformation: none of these are invented fears. But each of them points toward the same conclusion: we need to use AI better, not less. The answer to a flawed tool is a sharper user, better design, and stronger governance. It is not retreat.</p>

  <p class="reveal">As Andrew Mautner and Luke Tota concluded in their business presentation, the future belongs to organizations and people who use AI to amplify human judgment. I believe that completely. AI is not going to think for us. It is going to give us more time, more clarity, and more capacity to think for ourselves. What we do with that is up to us.</p>

  <p class="reveal" style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif; font-size:1.3rem; font-style:italic; color:var(--gold);">The tool is in our hands. The question is whether we are willing to use it well.</p>
</div>
</section>

